Sheets: the optimal row height pass measures each font once and keeps one height per row (large workbooks open faster).
After loading a workbook, ScDocShell::UpdateAllRowHeights measures every cell whose height cannot be taken
from its format alone, which is every cell in a range with a conditional format. Two costs grew with the size
of the sheet:
- Every cell set its font on the measuring device and asked for the text height. Neighbouring cells rarely
  share a format in large exported workbooks, so the device looked the font up again for nearly every cell:
  37 % of the row height pass on a synthetic 48 MB workbook (300 000 rows, 4000 formats, conditional formats).
  A cell shown on one line (no wrap, no rotation, standard orientation, no edit text, one script, not a
  running formula) is as high as its font's text height plus its margins, whatever the text says. That text
  height is now kept per (format, conditional style, table format, script) for one pass
  (sc/inc/hsttextheightcache.hxx); the cache holds a reference to each format it uses as a key.
- The heights collected by the pass were a run-length array; rows of different heights made it one entry per
  row, and each change in the middle moved the rest of the array (9 % of the pass, growing with the square of
  the rows). The pass now keeps one slot per row (sc/inc/hstrowheights.hxx) behind the same reading interface;
  its "runs" may end before the real stretch of equal heights, which the callers only step over.
Every row gets the same height as before: CppunitTest_sc_ucalc_condformat
testOptimalRowHeightsOnePassEqualsRowByRow compares one pass with row-by-row passes, and the row heights of
the large-file fixtures are compared before/after (scripts/perf). The one-slot array was checked against the
run-length array on 400 random passes: same heights, same merged ranges, same minimum-height start.
License: the new files sc/inc/hstrowheights.hxx and sc/inc/hsttextheightcache.hxx are HST Office code
(LicenseRef-HST-Proprietary); the modified upstream files keep their license (MPL-2.0).
